            0.148
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Find the area of a quadrant of a circle whose circumference is 66 cm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.155,
            0.886,
            0.215
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. The wheel of a car has an outer radius of 28 cm. Calculate how far the car travels after one complete turn of the wheel, and how many times the wheel turns during a journey of 1 km."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.221,
            0.885,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "*10. Two rectangles have the same area and the same perimeter. Does this mean that they are congruent to each other?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.269,
            0.886,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11. You know that the area of a parallelogram is base × height. Using this and the figure, show that the area of a trapezium is half the sum of the parallel sides × height, i.e., "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } ( a + b ) h ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.354,
            0.706,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.294,
                0.354,
                0.706,
                0.539
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_32_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.308,
                0.542,
                0.691,
                0.56
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.42: Trapezium: sides a and b, height h"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.578,
            0.885,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12. By dividing a trapezium into two triangles show that its area is, half the sum of the parallel sides multiplied by the height (the same formula as the one given above)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.646,
            0.887,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13. Show how we can use two identical copies of a trapezium to make a parallelogram. How will this give us the formula for the area of a trapezium?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.714,
            0.884,
            0.756
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "14. Show that the area of a kite is half the product of its diagonals. Show this: (i) using algebra, and (ii) using geometry."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.763,
            0.799,
            0.785
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "15. Three problems about fitting congruent shapes together:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.791,
            0.886,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Rectangle ABCD has sides a, b, and rectangle PQRS has sides 2a, 2b. Show that PQRS has 4 times the area of ABCD. Does this mean that 4 copies of rectangle ABCD will fit into rectangle PQRS? Check and see!"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
